-- Q1: medium / L3 — Two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinecrest Holdings has net income of $2,400,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025, with 400,000 weighted-average common shares and 100,000 shares of participating preferred stock entitled to share ratably in undistributed earnings. Declared preferred dividends total $200,000. Under the two-class method, what is Pinecrest''s basic EPS?', '["$5.50", "$5.00", "$4.40", "$6.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, entities with participating securities must use the two-class method for basic EPS. After deducting $200,000 declared preferred dividends, the remaining $2,200,000 of undistributed earnings is allocated proportionally: common receives 400,000/500,000 × $2,200,000 = $1,760,000, yielding basic EPS of $4.40. Choice A ($5.50) is incorrect because it deducts preferred dividends but fails to allocate undistributed earnings to the participating preferred shares as required by the two-class method.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Simple vs complex capital structure definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what distinguishes a complex capital structure from a simple capital structure?', '["A complex capital structure has more than one class of common stock outstanding", "A complex capital structure includes potentially dilutive securities such as options, warrants, or convertible instruments", "A complex capital structure requires consolidated financial statements", "A complex capital structure means the entity has both debt and equity financing"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding potentially dilutive securities—such as stock options, warrants, convertible bonds, or convertible preferred stock—that could reduce EPS upon conversion or exercise. A simple capital structure has no such securities. Choice D is incorrect because having both debt and equity financing does not create a complex capital structure; only the presence of potentially dilutive securities triggers that classification.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Anti-dilution sequencing with three securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northbrook Industries reports net income of $1,500,000 with 500,000 weighted-average common shares (basic EPS $3.00). Three potentially dilutive securities exist: (1) stock options adding 25,000 incremental shares, (2) 6% convertible bonds adding $60,000 after-tax interest and 40,000 shares, and (3) convertible preferred adding $100,000 dividends and 35,000 shares. Evaluate the order of inclusion and determine diluted EPS.', '["$2.56", "$2.67", "$2.83", "$2.76"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities are ranked by per-share impact (lowest first). Options ($0.00 impact) are most dilutive: $1,500,000/525,000 = $2.857. Bonds ($1.50 per share) are next: $1,560,000/565,000 = $2.76, still dilutive. Preferred ($2.857 per share) tested last: $1,660,000/600,000 = $2.77, which exceeds $2.76 and is antidilutive. Diluted EPS is $2.76. Choice A is incorrect because including the antidilutive preferred would improperly increase diluted EPS above $2.76.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: easy / L1 — Purpose of weighted-average shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What is the purpose of using weighted-average common shares outstanding in the EPS calculation?', '["To report the maximum number of shares outstanding during the period", "To reflect the time-weighted proportion of shares outstanding during the reporting period", "To include all authorized but unissued shares in the computation", "To adjust for the par value of shares issued at different prices"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding reflects shares weighted by the fraction of the reporting period during which they were outstanding. This ensures EPS reflects the actual capital available to generate earnings throughout the period. Choice A is incorrect because the maximum number of shares ignores the time shares were outstanding—shares issued near year-end contributed almost no capital to the period''s earnings.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q5: medium / L2 — Convertible preferred if-converted numerator add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Corp. reports net income of $1,000,000 with 250,000 weighted-average common shares and 5,000 shares of $100 par, 8% convertible preferred stock, each share convertible into 5 common shares. Basic EPS is $3.84. Using the if-converted method, what is diluted EPS?', '["$3.64", "$3.84", "$3.48", "$4.00"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible preferred stock adds back preferred dividends to the numerator ($40,000, restoring net income to $1,000,000) and adds shares issuable upon conversion (25,000) to the denominator (275,000 total). Diluted EPS = $1,000,000 / 275,000 = $3.64. This is dilutive because it is below basic EPS of $3.84. Choice B ($3.84) is incorrect because it represents basic EPS without considering the conversion feature of the preferred stock.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q6: hard / L4 — Rights issue below fair value (bonus element)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Havenbrook Inc. has 600,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025. On April 1, the company issues rights allowing purchase of one new share for every four held at $15 per share when the market price is $25. All rights are exercised on May 1. Net income for 2025 is $1,800,000. The theoretical ex-rights fair value is $23. Evaluate how the rights issue affects weighted-average shares and determine basic EPS.', '["$2.40", "$2.57", "$2.51", "$3.00"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-21, a rights issue priced below fair value contains a bonus element requiring adjustment. The factor equals pre-exercise fair value ($25) divided by the theoretical ex-rights price ($23), or 1.087. Pre-rights shares: 600,000 × 1.087 = 652,174 for 4 months. Post-rights: 750,000 for 8 months. Weighted-average = 717,391, yielding EPS of $2.51. Choice B ($2.57) is incorrect because it uses the straight weighted-average of 700,000 without applying the mandatory bonus element factor to pre-rights shares.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — EPS presentation for discontinued operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Caldwell Industries reports income from continuing operations of $1,200,000 and a loss from discontinued operations of $300,000 (net of tax) for the year ended December 31, 2025. Weighted-average common shares are 400,000 with no preferred stock. How should Caldwell present EPS on its income statement?', '["Report only a single EPS figure of $2.25 for net income", "Report EPS of $3.00 for continuing operations only, with no EPS for net income", "Report continuing operations EPS of $3.00 on the face and disclose net income EPS of $2.25 only in the notes", "Report continuing operations EPS of $3.00 and net income EPS of $2.25 on the face, with discontinued operations EPS on the face or in the notes"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, entities must present basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement for both income from continuing operations ($3.00) and net income ($2.25). EPS for discontinued operations (−$0.75) may be presented on the face or disclosed in the notes. Choice A is incorrect because reporting only net income EPS omits the required separate presentation of continuing operations EPS, which helps users assess recurring earnings power.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Restricted stock units in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenwood Corp. has 300,000 weighted-average common shares and net income of $1,500,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025. The company granted 15,000 restricted stock units on January 1, 2025, with a two-year vesting period. Average unrecognized compensation cost during 2025 is $180,000 and the average market price is $60 per share. Using the treasury stock method, how many incremental shares from the RSUs are included in diluted EPS?', '["15,000", "12,000", "3,000", "7,500"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, unvested restricted stock units are treated as options under the treasury stock method. The assumed proceeds equal the average unrecognized compensation cost ($180,000). These proceeds hypothetically repurchase shares at the average market price: $180,000 / $60 = 3,000 shares. Incremental shares = 15,000 − 3,000 = 12,000. Choice A (15,000) is incorrect because it treats all RSUs as incremental without deducting the shares assumed repurchased from the unrecognized compensation proceeds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Reverse stock split retroactive treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stonebridge Corp. had 500,000 shares outstanding for the full year ended December 31, 2025, and performed a 1-for-5 reverse stock split on November 1, 2025. What weighted-average number of shares should Stonebridge use to calculate basic EPS for 2025?', '["100,000", "433,333", "166,667", "500,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, reverse stock splits are applied retroactively to the beginning of the earliest period presented, the same treatment as forward splits and stock dividends. All prior-period shares are restated. Therefore, Stonebridge uses 100,000 shares (500,000 ÷ 5) for the entire year. Choice B (433,333) is incorrect because it treats the reverse split prospectively from November 1, weighting 500,000 shares for 10 months and 100,000 for 2 months, which misstates the weighted-average.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: easy / L2 — Retroactive adjustment for post-period stock dividend
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fairmont Inc. had 200,000 weighted-average shares outstanding during the year ended December 31, 2025. On February 15, 2026, before the financial statements were issued, the board declared a 20% stock dividend. For 2025 EPS purposes, how many weighted-average shares should Fairmont report?', '["200,000", "220,000", "240,000", "233,333"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be retroactively reflected in the EPS computation. The 20% stock dividend increases weighted-average shares to 200,000 × 1.20 = 240,000 for all periods presented. Choice A (200,000) is incorrect because it ignores the retroactive adjustment requirement, which ensures comparability and reflects the current capital structure available to investors.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Share repurchase effect on EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashworth Corp. had 800,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025, and net income of $4,000,000. On July 1, 2025, Ashworth repurchased 200,000 shares at $50 per share. The repurchase had no effect on net income. What is the most likely effect of the share repurchase on basic EPS compared to a scenario with no repurchase?', '["EPS decreases because the cash outflow reduces available earnings", "EPS is unchanged because the repurchase only affects the balance sheet", "EPS decreases because fewer shares receive dividends", "EPS increases because the weighted-average shares decline while net income remains constant"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, repurchased shares reduce the weighted-average from 800,000 to 700,000 (800,000 for 6 months plus 600,000 for 6 months). With net income unchanged at $4,000,000, basic EPS increases from $5.00 to $5.71. Choice A is incorrect because cash spent on repurchases does not reduce net income—the shares are removed from equity, not the income statement—so EPS rises due to the smaller denominator.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: medium / L3 — Mandatorily redeemable preferred stock treatment
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Cedarview Corp. has net income of $2,000,000 and 400,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has 50,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock classified as a liability under ASC 480, with annual dividends of $6 per share treated as interest expense. How should these dividends be treated in calculating basic EPS?', '["Already reflected in net income as interest expense, so no further EPS numerator adjustment is needed", "Deducted from net income in the EPS numerator, the same as traditional preferred dividends", "Added back to net income because they are noncash financing charges", "Ignored entirely because the preferred stock is classified as a liability and excluded from EPS"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 480-10-25-4, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is classified as a liability, and its dividends are recognized as interest expense in the income statement. Because these amounts are already deducted in arriving at net income, no additional numerator adjustment is required for basic EPS under ASC 260. Choice B is incorrect because it would double-count the preferred dividends—once as interest expense in net income and again as a numerator deduction in the EPS calculation.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q13: hard / L4 — Interim period EPS does not sum to annual EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redwood Partners reports quarterly data for 2025: Q1 net income $300,000 (250,000 shares), Q2 net income $450,000 (250,000 shares), Q3 net income $200,000 (300,000 shares after July 1 issuance of 50,000 shares), Q4 net income $550,000 (300,000 shares). No preferred stock exists. Evaluate whether the sum of the four quarterly basic EPS figures ($1.20 + $1.80 + $0.67 + $1.83 = $5.50) equals annual basic EPS.', '["$5.50; the sum of quarterly EPS equals annual EPS", "$5.45; the sum of quarterly EPS does not necessarily equal annual EPS due to independent weighted-average calculations", "$5.00; annual EPS must be computed by averaging the four quarterly EPS figures", "$5.50; quarterly EPS figures must be retroactively adjusted to sum to annual EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2 and ASC 270-10-50-1, annual EPS is computed independently using the annual weighted-average shares (275,000), yielding $5.45. The sum of quarterly EPS ($5.50) typically differs because each quarter uses its own weighted-average denominator. ASC 260 does not require quarterly figures to reconcile to annual EPS. Choice A ($5.50) is incorrect because it assumes annual EPS equals the sum of quarterly EPS, ignoring that each quarter''s independent weighting creates inherent differences.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Convertible debt after-tax interest add-back
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Birchfield Corp. has net income of $2,000,000 and 500,000 weighted-average common shares. The company has $5,000,000 of 6% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond convertible into 20 shares. The tax rate is 25%. What after-tax interest amount is added back to the numerator when computing diluted EPS under the if-converted method?', '["$300,000", "$150,000", "$225,000", "$75,000"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, the if-converted method for convertible bonds requires adding back interest expense net of tax to the numerator. Interest = $5,000,000 × 6% = $300,000; after-tax amount = $300,000 × (1 − 0.25) = $225,000. The tax adjustment is necessary because eliminating interest expense would increase taxable income and income tax. Choice A ($300,000) is incorrect because it adds back the full pre-tax interest, overstating the numerator by ignoring the incremental tax from removing the interest deduction.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Which entities must present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare GAAP financial statements", "All entities with publicly traded equity securities", "Entities with more than 500 shareholders of record", "Entities whose common stock or potential common stock trades in a public market"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock (such as options, warrants, or convertible securities) traded in a public market. Private entities are not required to present EPS. Choice A is incorrect because nonpublic entities preparing GAAP financial statements are exempt from the EPS disclosure requirements unless they voluntarily choose to present EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Business combination shares weighted from acquisition date
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestline Corp. acquires Dunmore Industries on September 1, 2025, issuing 120,000 new common shares in the transaction. Crestline had 500,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2025, and made no other share transactions. What weighted-average share count should Crestline use for its 2025 basic EPS?', '["540,000", "620,000", "560,000", "500,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-13, shares issued in a business combination are included in the weighted-average from the acquisition date. The 120,000 shares issued September 1 are weighted for 4 months (September–December): 120,000 × 4/12 = 40,000. Adding to the 500,000 base shares yields 540,000. Choice B (620,000) is incorrect because it includes all 120,000 shares for the full year, ignoring that acquisition shares are only weighted from the date the entity obtains control.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Where EPS must be presented
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, where must basic and diluted EPS be reported by public companies?', '["In the notes to the financial statements only", "On the face of the income statement", "In the management discussion and analysis section", "On the statement of stockholders'' equity"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, public entities must present basic and diluted EPS for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement, not merely in the notes. This requirement ensures EPS figures are prominently visible to financial statement users. Choice A is incorrect because note disclosure alone would not satisfy ASC 260''s explicit requirement for face-of-statement presentation of EPS data.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: medium / L3 — Written put options effect on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Corp. has written put options obligating it to repurchase 40,000 of its own shares at $30 per share. The average market price during 2025 is $24 per share. Net income is $1,200,000 with 500,000 weighted-average shares. What is the effect of the written put options on diluted EPS?', '["The put options have no effect because they are out-of-the-money", "The put options are antidilutive and excluded from diluted EPS", "The put options add 10,000 incremental shares to the denominator, reducing diluted EPS to $2.35", "The put options reduce the numerator by the expected settlement cost"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. Using the reverse treasury stock method, the company assumes repurchasing 40,000 shares at $30 ($1,200,000), then uses those funds to buy 50,000 shares at the $24 market price. The net 10,000 incremental shares increase the denominator, reducing diluted EPS from $2.40 to $2.35. Choice A is incorrect because written puts are in-the-money and dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the market price, opposite to call options.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Definition of potentially dilutive securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what are potentially dilutive securities?', '["Securities that could reduce EPS if converted, exercised, or otherwise result in the issuance of common shares", "Securities whose market value has declined below their carrying amount", "Securities classified as available-for-sale on the balance sheet", "Securities that increase the weighted-average shares outstanding at the balance sheet date"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, potentially dilutive securities are instruments—such as stock options, warrants, convertible bonds, and convertible preferred stock—that could decrease EPS or increase loss per share if exercised or converted. The term refers to potential dilution of existing shareholders'' earnings. Choice D is incorrect because it describes actual share issuances rather than securities with the potential to result in additional shares upon future conversion or exercise.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: hard / L4 — EPS restatement for prior-period error correction
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornton Industries discovered in 2025 that it had overstated 2024 net income by $400,000 due to an inventory error. The company has 250,000 weighted-average shares in both years and no preferred stock. Originally reported 2024 basic EPS was $6.00 (based on $1,500,000 net income). Evaluate how the error correction affects comparative EPS presentation in the 2025 financial statements.', '["No change to 2024 EPS; the correction is reported only in 2025 current-year EPS", "2024 EPS is adjusted to $4.80 and the difference is included in 2025 EPS", "2024 comparative EPS is restated to $5.20, reflecting a partial correction", "2024 comparative EPS is restated to $4.40, and 2025 EPS reflects only 2025 operations"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-1 and ASC 260-10-45-2, prior-period error corrections require retrospective restatement of all affected financial data, including EPS. The corrected 2024 net income ($1,100,000 ÷ 250,000 = $4.40) replaces the originally reported $6.00 in comparative presentations. Current-year 2025 EPS reflects only 2025 activity. Choice A is incorrect because ASC 250 requires restatement of prior-period amounts when presented comparatively, not prospective-only adjustment of the error.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q21: medium / L2 — Contingently issuable shares in diluted EPS (conditions not met)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bellamy Corp. has 300,000 weighted-average common shares and 25,000 contingently issuable shares that vest only if cumulative revenue exceeds $50 million by December 31, 2026. As of December 31, 2025, cumulative revenue is $38 million. How should the contingent shares be treated in the 2025 diluted EPS calculation?', '["Include all 25,000 shares in the diluted EPS denominator", "Exclude the contingent shares from both basic and diluted EPS", "Include a pro-rata portion of 19,000 shares based on the progress ratio", "Include 25,000 shares in basic EPS but exclude them from diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-51, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS only if the contingency conditions would be satisfied assuming the reporting date were the end of the contingency period. Since cumulative revenue of $38 million does not meet the $50 million threshold, the shares are excluded from diluted EPS and basic EPS. Choice C is incorrect because ASC 260 does not allow pro-rata inclusion—the contingency condition must be fully satisfied or the shares are entirely excluded.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q22: easy / L1 — Cumulative preferred dividends in arrears
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, how are cumulative preferred dividends in arrears treated in the basic EPS computation?', '["Dividends in arrears are ignored until declared by the board of directors", "Dividends in arrears are deducted from net income only in the year they are paid", "Cumulative preferred dividends are deducted from net income regardless of whether they have been declared", "Dividends in arrears are added to the denominator as additional equivalent shares"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-11, cumulative preferred dividends are deducted from net income (or added to net loss) in computing basic EPS for the period, whether or not the dividends have been declared. This reflects the preferred shareholders'' prior claim on earnings. Choice A is incorrect because it applies the treatment for noncumulative preferred stock—where only declared dividends are deducted—to cumulative preferred stock, which requires deduction regardless of declaration.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q23: hard / L4 — Two-class method vs if-converted for convertible participating preferred
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kensington Holdings has 400,000 common shares, net income of $3,600,000, and 50,000 shares of $40 par, 10% convertible preferred stock that participates equally in undistributed earnings. Each preferred share converts into 2 common shares. Evaluate whether the two-class method or the if-converted method produces a lower diluted EPS and identify which method should be used.', '["If-converted method ($7.20) is more dilutive and should be used for diluted EPS", "Two-class method ($7.56) is more dilutive and should be used for diluted EPS", "Both methods produce identical results for convertible participating preferred stock", "The if-converted method is prohibited for participating securities"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-60A, for securities that are both convertible and participating, the entity must use the more dilutive of the two-class or if-converted method. The if-converted method yields $7.20 ($3,600,000 ÷ 500,000 shares) versus the two-class method at $7.56 ($3,022,222 ÷ 400,000). Since $7.20 is lower, the if-converted method is more dilutive and must be used. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 260 explicitly requires testing both methods for convertible participating securities.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Incremental shares for warrants using treasury stock method
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Harborside Inc. has 250,000 weighted-average common shares and 30,000 warrants outstanding with an exercise price of $15 per share. The average market price during 2025 was $36 per share. Using the treasury stock method, what is the total number of shares used in the diluted EPS denominator?', '["280,000", "262,500", "237,500", "267,500"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method assumes warrants are exercised at the beginning of the period, generating proceeds of 30,000 × $15 = $450,000. These proceeds hypothetically repurchase 12,500 shares at the $36 average market price. Incremental shares = 30,000 − 12,500 = 17,500, giving a diluted denominator of 267,500. Choice A (280,000) is incorrect because it adds all 30,000 warrant shares without deducting the shares assumed repurchased with the exercise proceeds.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: medium / L2 — Diluted EPS control number (loss from continuing operations)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fernwood Corp. reports a loss from continuing operations of $500,000 and income from discontinued operations of $200,000, resulting in a net loss of $300,000. The company has 100,000 weighted-average common shares and dilutive stock options that would add 3,000 incremental shares. Which amount serves as the control number for determining whether potentially dilutive securities are included?', '["Net loss of $300,000", "Loss from continuing operations of $500,000", "Income from discontinued operations of $200,000", "The absolute value of net loss of $300,000"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, income (or loss) from continuing operations serves as the control number for determining whether potentially dilutive securities are included in diluted EPS. Because Fernwood has a loss from continuing operations, all potentially dilutive securities are antidilutive and must be excluded from diluted EPS for all line items. Choice A is incorrect because net loss is not the control number—using net loss could yield a different inclusion decision when discontinued operations change the sign of the result.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q26: hard / L3 — Forward contract to repurchase own shares
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stratton Corp. entered a forward contract on March 1, 2025, obligating it to repurchase 50,000 of its own shares at $45 per share on March 1, 2026. The average market price during 2025 is $36 per share. Net income is $3,000,000 with 600,000 weighted-average common shares. Analyze the effect of this forward contract on diluted EPS.', '["Diluted EPS is $4.90; the forward adds 12,500 incremental shares to the denominator", "Diluted EPS is $5.00; the forward has no effect because it has not yet settled", "Diluted EPS is $4.62; all 50,000 forward shares reduce the denominator", "Diluted EPS is $4.80; the forward reduces the numerator by the settlement premium"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, forward repurchase contracts where the repurchase price exceeds the average market price are dilutive. The $2,250,000 outflow (50,000 × $45) could purchase 62,500 shares at the $36 market price, creating 12,500 net incremental shares. Diluted EPS = $3,000,000 ÷ 612,500 = $4.90. Choice B is incorrect because ASC 260 requires assumed settlement of forward contracts regardless of whether the settlement date has passed, similar to the treatment of stock options.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Anti-dilution rule: each issue tested independently
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Prescott Corp. has basic EPS of $3.00 based on net income of $900,000 and 300,000 shares. Two potentially dilutive securities exist: Security A adds $24,000 to the numerator and 10,000 shares (per-share impact $2.40), and Security B adds $36,000 to the numerator and 10,000 shares (per-share impact $3.60). What is the correct diluted EPS?', '["$3.00; both securities are excluded because their combined effect is antidilutive", "$2.85; both securities are included in diluted EPS", "$2.98; only Security A is included because Security B is antidilutive after including A", "$2.94; only Security B is included because it has the larger per-share impact"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-21, potentially dilutive securities are tested individually in order of per-share dilutive impact (lowest first). Security A ($2.40 per share) is tested first and is dilutive: ($900,000 + $24,000) / (300,000 + 10,000) = $2.98. Security B ($3.60 per share) exceeds the running diluted EPS of $2.98 and is therefore antidilutive and excluded. Diluted EPS is $2.98. Choice A is incorrect because Security A is individually dilutive and must be included even though Security B is excluded.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what is the definition of an antidilutive security?', '["A security that reduces the weighted-average shares outstanding", "A security with a market value below its conversion price", "A security that has been retired or redeemed during the period", "A security whose inclusion in the EPS calculation would increase EPS or decrease loss per share"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, an antidilutive security is one whose inclusion in the diluted EPS computation would increase earnings per share or decrease loss per share compared to basic EPS. Such securities are excluded from diluted EPS because their effect would overstate per-share performance. Choice B is incorrect because a security''s market value relative to its conversion price determines whether it is in-the-money, not whether it is antidilutive—the antidilution test depends on the effect on the computed EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q29: medium / L3 — Performance shares with unmet performance condition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Waverly Corp. granted 20,000 performance shares on January 1, 2025, vesting upon achieving a 15% return on equity by December 31, 2026. As of December 31, 2025, the return on equity is 12% and average unrecognized compensation cost is $240,000. The average market price is $48 per share. How should these performance shares be treated for 2025 diluted EPS?', '["Include 15,000 shares after applying the treasury stock method with assumed proceeds", "Exclude the performance shares because the performance condition is not satisfied at year-end", "Include all 20,000 shares because they were granted during the period", "Include a proportional 16,000 shares based on the 12%/15% progress toward the target"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares based on performance conditions are included in diluted EPS only if the conditions would have been satisfied assuming the reporting date were the end of the contingency period. Since the 12% return on equity does not meet the 15% target at December 31, 2025, the performance shares are excluded. Choice D is incorrect because ASC 260 does not permit pro-rata inclusion based on progress—the performance threshold is an all-or-nothing test for EPS purposes.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — Basic EPS formula components
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'What are the two components of the basic earnings per share formula under ASC 260?', '["Net income minus preferred dividends in the numerator and weighted-average common shares in the denominator", "Net income in the numerator and total shares authorized in the denominator", "Operating income in the numerator and shares outstanding at year-end in the denominator", "Comprehensive income in the numerator and weighted-average common and preferred shares in the denominator"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, basic EPS equals net income available to common shareholders (net income minus preferred dividends) divided by the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding during the period. This formula isolates earnings attributable to common shareholders. Choice C is incorrect because operating income excludes nonoperating items and taxes, and year-end shares outstanding ignores the time-weighted capital contributions throughout the period.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
